mudlle: MUD Language for Little Extensions
------------------------------------------

This software is Copyright (C) 1993-2012 David Gay and Gustav Hållberg.
Read the COPYRIGHT file for terms of use.


Installation
------------
To compile on Linux or macOS:

  make
  make compiler

Linux Setup and Backend Validation
----------------------------------
On Debian or Ubuntu, install the build tools and LLVM development files:

  sudo apt install build-essential flex bison libgmp-dev libreadline-dev llvm llvm-dev

The build detects LLVM through `llvm-config`; normal JIT execution does not
need the LLVM command-line tools. Keep the LLVM headers, C API library, and
`llvm-config` from the same installation:

  llvm-config --version

Build each x86-64 backend from a clean tree. The clean step matters because
the generated compiler objects are backend-specific:

  make clean
  make
  make compiler

  make clean
  make USE_LLVM=yes

Run the same regression suite in each build:

  ./mudlle -e xc.mud -e regression/all.mud

The suite should print `SUCCESS`. It includes the LLVM-specific checks when
the LLVM backend is selected. Run `make compiler USE_LLVM=yes` to check the
multi-stage compiler bootstrap as well. The validation evidence below records
the bootstrap status separately from the language regression suite.

To compare execution after compilation, run the compiled-kernel benchmark:

  ./mudlle -e xc.mud -e regression/benchmark.mud

The benchmark compiles and links one function before starting its timer, checks
the result, then runs a compiled integer reduction loop 2,000 times at 50,000
iterations per call. Its `cpu_ms` value measures Mudlle process CPU time, not
compiler/JIT startup. LLVM runs the `default<O2>` optimization pipeline by
default. Set `MUDLLE_LLVM_OPT=none`, `O0`, `O1`, `O2`, or `O3` to compare
optimization levels. Run each backend from a clean build, repeat the command
several times, and compare the median. `regression/all.mud` is a correctness
suite; timing that whole command also includes loading the compiler, compiling
test snippets, and JIT setup.

macOS Setup
-----------
On macOS (Homebrew), install the required toolchain dependencies including LLVM:

  brew install llvm flex bison

Ensure `llvm-config` for Homebrew LLVM is available to the build:

  export PATH="$(brew --prefix llvm)/bin:$PATH"


Quick Introduction
------------------

mudlle is a small language designed for writing extensions to MUME, an
online multi-user game. It is heavily inspired by Scheme. This
distribution contains a stand-alone interpreter and compiler that can
be used to experiment with the language. It is up to you to integrate
it with your favourite application if you wish to use it as an
extension language.

Once compiled, run mudlle and check that basic stuff works:

  ./mudlle
  mudlle> 2+3
  Result: 5
  mudlle>

EOF (Ctrl-D), or evaluating quit() will exit the interpreter.

doc/mudlle-intro.doc and doc/mudlle-ref.doc contain a language introduction
and reference. doc/llvm-backend.md documents the LLVM backend.

LLVM Compiler Demo (Apple Silicon)
----------------------------------
Install LLVM and make its tools visible before building:

  brew install llvm
  export PATH="$(brew --prefix llvm)/bin:$PATH"
  make

On ARM64 and RISC-V, `load("xc.mud")` selects the LLVM backend. At the prompt,
compile a function to LLVM IR text:

  load("xc.mud")
  source = "fn (a, b) a + b";
  tree = mudlle_parse(source, null, false);
  ir = mc:compile_llvm(tree, 1, mc:source_code(source));
  w = writer:new_file("module.ll");
  writer:write(w, ir);
  writer:close(w);

The IR can be inspected or validated with optional LLVM command-line tools.
ORC LLJIT sets the target triple and data layout from the host at runtime. On
x86-64, `load("xc.mud")` keeps the legacy compiler; load `llvmc.mud` to select
LLVM explicitly. Start a fresh Mudlle process with each loader when comparing
the backends.

Without LLVM installed, the interpreter remains available. Native x86-64
compilation continues to use the existing backend.

Validation and Compatibility
----------------------------
Linux x86-64 validation on 2026-10-11: GCC 14.2.0 (Debian 14.2.0-19),
LLVM 19.1.7, AMD Ryzen 9 7940HS. Mudlle execution remains single threaded.

* Clean x64 runtime and three-stage compiler builds succeed, and the full
  regression suite prints `SUCCESS`.
* LLVM passes the full suite with the source compiler under default O2 and
  with `MUDLLE_LLVM_OPT=none`. The corrected three-stage bootstrap succeeds
  with `MUDLLE_LLVM_OPT=none`; its final compiled compiler loads under default
  O2 and the full suite prints `SUCCESS`.
* LLVM 14 opaque-pointer compatibility was validated on 2026-10-10; it has
  not been revalidated after these changes. Earlier Apple Silicon validation
  passed `regression/llvm.mud`. ARM64 and RISC-V runtime compatibility still
  need direct validation.
* Remaining parity gaps include synthetic callback names/source locations,
  ORC modules retained for the process lifetime, unsupported `global_syms`,
  and compiler startup performance. Float and bigint arithmetic dispatches
  through Mudlle primitives. LLVM and x64 serialized code formats differ.

LLVM emits module-local tag guards for integer operations and promotes scalar
locals to SSA when no generated operation can collect. Allocating functions
retain GC roots and reload their closure environment; variadic entry setup is
also allocating. The loop counter uses atomic monotonic load/store operations
compatible with the signal handler. To inspect the scalar kernel's IR:

    ./mudlle -e xc.mud -e regression/llvm-benchmark-ir.mud
    opt -passes='default<O2>' -S /tmp/mudlle-benchmark.ll \
      -o /tmp/mudlle-benchmark.opt.ll

Quick benchmark (2026-10-11)
----------------------------
Three sequential samples per configuration on the host above, with default
GCC -O2 runtime builds. Fresh processes, warm filesystem caches, no CPU pinning.
The kernel performs 2,000 calls of 50,000 integer reduction iterations; its
CPU timer starts after compilation/linking. Compiler load timing measures a
fresh process loading the final `icxc.mud` compiler and checking an evaluation
of `iadd(20,22)`. It includes object loading, JIT work, and that small evaluation.

| Configuration | Kernel CPU samples (ms) | Median (ms) | Compiler load wall samples (s) | Median (s) |
|---------------|-------------------------|-------------|-------------------------------|------------|
| x64           | 115, 105, 96            | 105         | 0.0438, 0.0471, 0.0579        | 0.0471     |
| LLVM O2       | 52, 40, 45              | 45          | 66.1473, 67.3429, 67.1650     | 67.1650    |
| LLVM none     | 419, 415, 410           | 415         | 7.4374, 7.4747, 7.0611        | 7.4374     |

LLVM O2 executes this scalar workload about 2.3 times as fast as x64, while
compiled compiler loading is about 1,426 times slower. Disabling the optional
IR optimization pipeline reduces LLVM loading time about 9 times, but slows
this kernel about 9.2 times relative to O2. These small samples do not establish
broad execution parity or identify the individual startup phases. The current
LLVM path recompiles saved IR on each restart; persistent native object caching
is the next performance task. Source metadata and other parity gaps remain.

All samples, including whole-process user/system/wall times, are recorded in
`doc/benchmarks/2026-10-11-llvm-x64.json`. Reproduce with separately built x64
and LLVM trees containing their final compiler objects:

    python3 regression/benchmark-backends.py \
      --x64 /path/to/x64-build --llvm /path/to/llvm-build --runs 3 \
      > /tmp/mudlle-backend-benchmark.json

The runner checks workload completion because process exit status alone does
not detect every Mudlle load error. It runs samples sequentially and explicitly
selects O2 or none for LLVM, ignoring an inherited `MUDLLE_LLVM_OPT` setting.

Resolved LLVM bootstrap failures (2026-10-11)
-------------------------------------------

The old build1/.comp-0 GC assertion was traced with GDB outside the ptrace
sandbox. The six-element vector was marked readonly and immutable, but its
children were still movable vectors. After promotion to generation 1, minor
collections skipped its fields and left stale pointers into generation 0.
`llvm_build_vector` now checks every element before setting OBJ_IMMUTABLE.
The regression covers a readonly vector of nested constants across collections.

Once that corruption was fixed, bootstrap exposed duplicate SSA names for
coalesced or discarded arguments in phase2. Incoming loads now use argument
positions for names and keep slot numbers only for storage. The regression
checks a function with several unused arguments.

Native dispatch and slot reuse address the next recursion failures. LLVM calls
previously re-entered the bytecode interpreter, and GC-visible stack slots
could not be reused by LLVM because their addresses escaped to C. The generator
now colors local slots using the existing compiler liveness analysis. Slot zero
remains reserved for discarded values, including unused argument-count writes.
Indirect variables have exclusive slots. Builtin code generation uses separate
guards instead of a deeply nested else-if chain.

The C callback constructor also protects its callback object before allocating
its string cache. Numeric fallback comparisons reload their rooted operation
name after calling a primitive that can move it.

The final compiler initially rejected `mc:get_loc` as no longer immutable.
`compiler_stable_closure?` had incorrectly declared a bigint result (`x.b`)
while returning an integer boolean. Inference therefore treated its false
result as truthy and recorded an invalid immutable dependency. Its signature
now matches the other boolean predicates (`x.n`), with a conditional regression.

Reproduce the Linux LLVM 19 validation in a fresh build directory:

    make USE_LLVM=yes
    MUDLLE_LLVM_OPT=none make compiler USE_LLVM=yes
    ./mudlle -c 'silent=true' -e icxc.mud -e regression/all.mud
    ./mudlle -c 'silent=true' -e xc.mud -e regression/all.mud

The bootstrap command disables the optional LLVM IR optimization passes;
the following commands use default O2. Check for the final `SUCCESS` sentinel:
a Mudlle load error can occur even when the process exits zero. Compiler objects
are backend-specific; use separate build directories for LLVM and x64.

Performance follow-up: the earlier default-O2 run completed the two compiled
bootstrap stages in 534.5 and 542.5 seconds, compared with 2.1 and 1.9 seconds
for a separate serial x64 build on the same host. These are checkpoint-to-
checkpoint wall times, including compiler loading and compilation, rather than
steady-state execution benchmarks. The corrected run with
`MUDLLE_LLVM_OPT=none` took 134.0 and 129.7 seconds for those stages; this
setting still performs JIT machine-code generation. LLVM continued advancing
through all eight slices; it was not hung. Investigate compiler loading/JIT
cost and compilation cost separately in the next session. ORC can reload
native object files;
`doc/llvm-backend.md` records the proposed cache and relocation work. Keep the
runtime single threaded.

